UNIT isString
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG isString(value: any) -> boolean
DEPENDS-ON none   # zero runtime deps; uses only JS built-ins Array.isArray and Object.prototype.toString

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PURPOSE
# Checks whether `value` is classified as a String — i.e. a string PRIMITIVE
# OR a boxed String object (new String('x')). Returns a strict boolean.
# This is lodash 4.0.1's `_.isString`, exported as a standalone module.
# Pure function: no seams (no clock / RNG / env / io / net). Fully deterministic.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

CONSTANTS
  stringTag      = '[object String]'            # exact literal; the Object#toString tag for String objects
  objectProto    = Object.prototype             # built-in reference
  objectToString = Object.prototype.toString    # the BORROWED toString (NOT the value's own toString)
  isArray        = Array.isArray                # built-in reference

BEHAVIOR   # exact pipeline — short-circuit OR; order matters
  # The function body is exactly:
  #   return typeof value == 'string' ||
  #     (!isArray(value) && isObjectLike(value) && objectToString.call(value) == stringTag);
  #
  # where  isObjectLike(value) := (!!value && typeof value == 'object')

  NODE 1  PRIMITIVE-STRING short-circuit
    IF typeof value === 'string'  -> RETURN true     # covers '', '  ', any literal/template string, unicode, '123', etc.
    # NOTE: typeof returns 'string' ONLY for primitive strings. Boxed String objects have typeof === 'object',
    #       so they FALL THROUGH to NODE 2. bigint -> 'bigint', number -> 'number', boolean -> 'boolean',
    #       symbol -> 'symbol', function -> 'function', undefined -> 'undefined' all fall through here and,
    #       failing NODE 2, return false.

  NODE 2  BOXED-STRING / object-tag path  (a 3-way AND; evaluated left-to-right, short-circuits on first false)
    # 2a  !isArray(value)
    #     IF Array.isArray(value) is true  -> the whole AND is false -> (NODE 1 already false) RETURN false.
    #     Arrays are explicitly excluded here so [] / [1,2,3] never reach the tag check.
    # 2b  isObjectLike(value)  ==  ( !!value && typeof value == 'object' )
    #     - !!value is false for null (typeof null is 'object' but null is falsy) -> RETURN false for null.
    #     - typeof value == 'object' is false for number/boolean/bigint/symbol/function/undefined -> RETURN false.
    #     - true ONLY for non-null objects: plain objects, boxed primitives (String/Number/Boolean objects),
    #       Date, RegExp, Map, Set, Error, typed arrays, Buffer, arrays (but arrays already excluded at 2a).
    # 2c  objectToString.call(value) == '[object String]'
    #     Uses the BORROWED Object.prototype.toString applied to `value` (objectToString.call(value)).
    #     This is the brand/tag check. It yields '[object String]' for:
    #       - boxed String objects:  new String('x') / new String('')   -> '[object String]'  -> RETURN true
    #       - any object whose effective Symbol.toStringTag resolves to 'String'
    #         (e.g. a plain object with value[Symbol.toStringTag] === 'String') -> '[object String]' -> RETURN true
    #     It yields a DIFFERENT tag (RETURN false) for everything else:
    #       - plain object {}                       -> '[object Object]'
    #       - boxed Number new Number(5)            -> '[object Number]'
    #       - boxed Boolean new Boolean(true)       -> '[object Boolean]'
    #       - Date                                  -> '[object Date]'
    #       - RegExp                                -> '[object RegExp]'
    #       - Map / Set                             -> '[object Map]' / '[object Set]'
    #       - Uint8Array / typed array              -> '[object Uint8Array]'
    #       - Buffer (a Uint8Array subclass)        -> '[object Uint8Array]'
    #       - Error                                 -> '[object Error]'
    #     CRITICAL: this uses Object.prototype.toString, NOT value.toString(). A plain object that overrides
    #     its OWN toString to return the literal string '[object String]'  STILL returns FALSE, because the
    #     borrowed Object.prototype.toString brands it as '[object Object]'. (Verified against the real package.)
    #
    #   If all of 2a && 2b && 2c are true -> RETURN true.  Otherwise -> RETURN false.

  RESULT  The function ALWAYS returns a strict boolean (true | false). It never throws for any input,
          including calling with no arguments (value === undefined -> false). It does not read or mutate
          any external state and is referentially transparent.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TRUTH TABLE (demonstrated, self-consistent with the input generator)
#   true:
#     'abc' , '' , '   ' , '123' , multiline/template strings , unicode 'café 🎉'
#     new String('x') , new String('')
#     plain object with Symbol.toStringTag === 'String'   # documented OUT of generator (see SCOPE) but a real true-branch
#   false:
#     0 , 1 , 3.14 , -0 , NaN , Infinity , -Infinity , new Number(5)
#     true , false , new Boolean(true)
#     null , undefined , (no argument)
#     42n (bigint) , Symbol('s')
#     [] , [1,2,3] , ['a','b']
#     {} , {a:1} , Object.create(null) , object with own toString returning '[object String]'
#     /re/ , new Date() , new Map() , new Set() , new Uint8Array(3) , Buffer.from('hi')
#     function(){} , ()=>{}
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

ORACLE value -> oracle.json
  # Pure deterministic value oracle: for each generated [value], record isString(value) (a boolean).
  # No seam injection required. Throw-paths: none exist (the function cannot throw on any codec-faithful input).

SCOPE
  IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly):
    - The full short-circuit pipeline of NODE 1 and NODE 2 above, in order.
    - The exact tag literal '[object String]'.
    - The use of the BORROWED Object.prototype.toString (not value's own toString).
    - The explicit array exclusion via Array.isArray BEFORE the tag check.
    - The isObjectLike null/typeof guard.
    - Strict boolean return; never throws.
  CARRIED DATA: none. There is no large literal table; the only literal is the 15-char tag string
    '[object String]', which is reproduced inline in the BEHAVIOR (it is a JS-engine brand, not a carried table).
    No carried.json is emitted.
  OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (documented; NOT emitted by genInputs because they cannot survive the JSON.stringify(encode(args))
                   codec round-trip, but a blind re-emitter reconstructs their behavior from the BEHAVIOR spec):
    - A plain object carrying Symbol.toStringTag === 'String'  -> would return TRUE (NODE 2c). It is a real
      true-branch, but it is a symbol-keyed object property that does not round-trip through JSON, so it is
      specified here rather than generated. (The new String('x') case below covers the same true-branch with a
      codec-faithful value.)
    - Live functions, Symbols, and identity-bearing class instances whose own toString is overridden: their
      isString result is fully determined by the spec (all false except via the toStringTag brand), but they are
      not JSON-codec-faithful, so they are documented here and not generated.

